
Direct 1-to-1 Mapping

Spring Boot
@PostMapping("/users")
public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
    return userService.save(user);
}

Gin
func CreateUser(c *gin.Context) {
	var user models.User
	c.ShouldBindJSON(&user)
}


✔ Same purpose
✔ Same behavior
✔ Same responsibility

🧠 What ShouldBindJSON actually does

Behind the scenes, Gin:

Reads request body
Parses JSON
Maps JSON → struct fields
Runs validation (if tags exist)
Returns error if invalid

Equivalent to:
HttpMessageConverter + Jackson


Go struct
type User struct {
	Name  string `json:"name"`
	Email string `json:"email"`
}


Gin automatically maps:

name → Name
email → Email



Validation comparison (VERY IMPORTANT)
Spring Boot
public User create(@Valid @RequestBody User user) {}

Gin
type User struct {
	Email string `json:"email" binding:"required,email"`
}

c.ShouldBindJSON(&user)


✔ Same idea
✔ Struct tags replace annotations



| Gin                | Spring           |
| ------------------ | ---------------- |
| `ShouldBindJSON`   | `@RequestBody`   |
| `ShouldBindQuery`  | `@RequestParam`  |
| `ShouldBindUri`    | `@PathVariable`  |
| `ShouldBindHeader` | `@RequestHeader` |
| `ShouldBind`       | Auto detect      |





The MOST IMPORTANT Go rule (you must remember this)
🔴 Unexported fields (lowercase) are INVISIBLE outside the package
type User struct {
	name  string
	email string
}


❌ Gin cannot see them
❌ JSON cannot marshal/unmarshal them
❌ GORM cannot map them

Result:

{}


Or binding fails silently.

✅ Correct & idiomatic Go way
Struct fields → PascalCase
JSON keys → lowercase / snake_case
type User struct {
	Name  string `json:"name"`
	Email string `json:"email"`
}


✔ Fields are exported
✔ JSON stays clean
✔ DB mapping works
✔ Reflection works

🧠 Why Go enforces this (design philosophy)

Go uses capitalization instead of annotations like:

private / public


Name → public

name → private

This is:

Compile-time enforced

Fast

Simple

No reflection magic

❌ What you’re thinking (both lowercase) — why it breaks
This will NOT work:
type User struct {
	name  string `json:"name"`
	email string `json:"email"`
}


Because:

JSON encoder ignores unexported fields

Gin cannot bind request body

GORM cannot map columns

🟢 What IS good practice (industry standard)
✔ Struct (Go world)
Name  string
Email string

✔ API (JSON world)
{
  "name": "Piyush",
  "email": "piyush@gmail.com"
}

✔ DB (SQL world)
name, email


Different casing per layer is normal and expected.

🧩 Spring Boot comparison (why this feels weird)
Java
private String name;


Java:

Access controlled by keywords

Reflection can bypass private

Go:

Access controlled by naming

Reflection RESPECTS visibility

That’s why Go feels stricter — and safer.

🔥 Advanced tip (when lowercase IS acceptable)

Lowercase fields are fine for:

Internal structs

Helper structs

Not serialized

Not exposed

Example:

type cacheKey struct {
	userID int
}


But NOT for:

Request DTOs

Response models

DB models

✅ Final verdict (clear & firm)

❌ Lowercase struct fields for API/DB models = BAD
✅ PascalCase fields + json:"lowercase" = BEST PRACTICE

You were right to question this — but Go’s rules are different from Java.



